\paragraph{Degeneracy is model-family-dependent, not scale-dependent.}
Qwen2.5 exhibits systematically higher degeneracy than Qwen3 on Few-NERD (7B--72B: $31.7$--$46.2$\% vs.\ Qwen3-8B: $13.0$\%; Table~\ref{tab:scale_comparison}), confirming degeneracy is shaped by architecture rather than parameter count. Construction fails for all Qwen2.5 configurations on Few-NERD (both n.s.); 3$\to$5 epochs triples degeneracy within the same model (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).

\paragraph{Qwen3 8B vs.\ 4B scale ablation.}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scale ablation: Qwen3 8B vs.\ 4B QE $\rho$ ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, seed 42). RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:scale_ablation_app}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll cc r@{}}
\toprule
& & \textbf{8B} & \textbf{4B} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & SJ & .436 & .422 & $-$.014 \\
      & FK & .400 & .418 & $+$.018 \\
SciERC & SJ & .360 & .422 & $+$.062 \\
       & FK & .267 & .326 & $+$.059 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Degeneracy scales with capability: 8B shows 12.2\% vs.\ 4B's 10.4\% on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:degeneracy}).

\paragraph{14B scale extension.}
Qwen3-14B fine-tuned on Few-NERD achieves greedy F1${=}0.798$ ($+0.9$\,pp over 8B) with oracle headroom of 8.1\,pp ($+1.2$\,pp over 8B's 6.9\,pp) and $\rho$ advantage $+0.029$ over 8B.
The ceiling grows with model capability: larger models improve both greedy and oracle performance, but the aggregation gap widens rather than closes.

\paragraph{Pre-trained baseline (no fine-tuning).}
Table~\ref{tab:pretrained_summary} summarizes pretrained-vs-finetuned MV performance across three datasets (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct pretrained, Qwen3-8B LoRA finetuned; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$).
Across all three datasets, pretrained models benefit from MV aggregation ($+1.7$ to $+9.2$\,pp), while all finetuned models show negligible or negative MV gains.

